WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.150 --> 00:00:02.889
Film Courage: Jill what are the five biggest
mistakes screenwriters make?

00:00:02.889 --> 00:00:05.320
Jill Chamberlain, Script Consultant/Author/Writer:
Well I don't know if I'm going to do five

00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:06.320
but off the top of my head?

00:00:06.320 --> 00:00:11.240
But I will say underestimating the importance
of structure.

00:00:11.240 --> 00:00:17.870
A good structure is like one, two, three and
four, right?

00:00:17.870 --> 00:00:20.870
It's going to paint the way.

00:00:20.870 --> 00:00:26.460
It is the story, structure is story.

00:00:26.460 --> 00:00:29.419
William Goldman is the one who said that.

00:00:29.419 --> 00:00:33.090
It is determining character development.

00:00:33.090 --> 00:00:41.250
So all of those...I really do think one, two,
three and four are the importance of structure.

00:00:41.250 --> 00:00:49.440
And then if I had to say fifth thing...I want
to say it's sort of contradictory thing that

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:56.850
you need to be both tougher on yourself and
you need to be easier on yourself.

00:00:56.850 --> 00:01:05.860
That I see writers do things like they’ll
say to a friend (maybe the friend is more

00:01:05.860 --> 00:01:10.040
accomplished writer than they are) and they
will say “I finished my draft, would you

00:01:10.040 --> 00:01:11.040
read it?

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:12.740
It’s not very good.”

00:01:12.740 --> 00:01:16.671
Why are you bothering your friend (a more
accomplished writer) to read your draft that

00:01:16.671 --> 00:01:18.520
is not very good?

00:01:18.520 --> 00:01:19.740
Right?

00:01:19.740 --> 00:01:22.210
You need to put a little more effort into
it.

00:01:22.210 --> 00:01:23.500
You need to be a little harder on yourself.

00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:28.540
You should think your draft is very good before
you are opening it up for feedback.

00:01:28.540 --> 00:01:32.950
You are kind of wasting the expertise of those
around you.

00:01:32.950 --> 00:01:35.890
You’re sharing drafts when you don’t think
they are very good.

00:01:35.890 --> 00:01:39.980
At the same time you kind of need to be easier
on yourself.

00:01:39.980 --> 00:01:43.979
So be harder on yourself in the sense that
don’t bug your friend until your script

00:01:43.979 --> 00:01:51.799
is in good place but also be kind to yourself
that there’s a lot of what makes people

00:01:51.799 --> 00:01:56.230
successful is luck, is dumb luck you know?

00:01:56.230 --> 00:02:00.510
There are people who are more talented than
you that are successful and there are people

00:02:00.510 --> 00:02:03.380
who are less talented than you that are successful.

00:02:03.380 --> 00:02:06.850
So give yourself a break that no script is
going to be perfect.

00:02:06.850 --> 00:02:11.629
Even a great writer can…and I’ve seen
writers kind of stress themselves out doing

00:02:11.629 --> 00:02:13.849
draft after draft after draft.

00:02:13.849 --> 00:02:18.189
And obsessing and getting contradictory notes
and then they don’t know what they are doing

00:02:18.189 --> 00:02:22.689
and then they are driving themselves crazy
because they are being a little too hard on

00:02:22.689 --> 00:02:23.689
themselves.

00:02:23.689 --> 00:02:26.310
At some point you’ve got to let it be.

00:02:26.310 --> 00:02:30.849
Once you feel good about it, you’ve gotten
some good drafts on it, you feel good, you’ve

00:02:30.849 --> 00:02:34.840
gotten some good feedback from your writing
group or people you trust, not everyone is

00:02:34.840 --> 00:02:35.870
going to like it.

00:02:35.870 --> 00:02:42.349
You’ve got to put it out in the world and
relax and you’re going to get some notes

00:02:42.349 --> 00:02:45.140
back that aren’t what you agree with.

00:02:45.140 --> 00:02:49.659
Don’t obsess about it because you can’t
please everyone.

00:02:49.659 --> 00:02:57.780
Film Courage: With your book The Nutshell
Technique, which chapter is most debated by

00:02:57.780 --> 00:03:01.559
people, whether it’s a friendly debate or
whether it’s outright where people have

00:03:01.559 --> 00:03:05.790
these severe beliefs about writing.

00:03:05.790 --> 00:03:11.920
Jill: I’ll say the most confusion is the
concept of the want.

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:17.279
So the very first thing I talk about is that
in the first scene the character has something

00:03:17.279 --> 00:03:21.219
that they want that they get in the point
of no return.

00:03:21.219 --> 00:03:27.139
This is the trickiest piece for some reason
and I have two chapters on it.

00:03:27.139 --> 00:03:31.269
It’s actually the only element I devote
two chapters to for this reason.

00:03:31.269 --> 00:03:38.290
I try to and this is just from years of experience
that I’ve learned this, I’ve tried to

00:03:38.290 --> 00:03:44.980
help people by calling it and explaining and
yet still there is confusion about this.

00:03:44.980 --> 00:03:49.099
This want that I’m talking about here, I
call it the set-up want.

00:03:49.099 --> 00:03:55.499
It’s not necessarily the character’s greatest
want.

00:03:55.499 --> 00:04:00.760
It is not necessarily the character’s biggest
motivation.

00:04:00.760 --> 00:04:03.200
And yet people still do that.

00:04:03.200 --> 00:04:09.510
They still think it’s that “Well my character
wants one thing and according to Jill’s

00:04:09.510 --> 00:04:13.159
Nutshell Technique they have to get it in
the point of no return.

00:04:13.159 --> 00:04:16.100
But that doesn’t work with my point of no
return.

00:04:16.100 --> 00:04:22.360
My point that I’ve tried to make clear is
that just because your character has multiple

00:04:22.360 --> 00:04:25.370
flaws, they have multiple things that they
want.

00:04:25.370 --> 00:04:29.800
There’s not a one-to-one relationship between
wants and point of no returns.

00:04:29.800 --> 00:04:32.349
Your character wants multiple things.

00:04:32.349 --> 00:04:38.099
All I’m saying is there is one want that
they get in the point-of-no-return.

00:04:38.099 --> 00:04:43.350
It may not be there biggest want, it may not
be their defining one, it may not be the thing

00:04:43.350 --> 00:04:48.250
that they say that they want, it’s just
something that they want.

00:04:48.250 --> 00:04:51.310
So sometimes I call it a throwaway want.

00:04:51.310 --> 00:04:54.080
Sometimes your character just wants a sandwich.

00:04:54.080 --> 00:04:57.940
All we’re doing is we’re setting up this
little piece “be careful what you wish for”

00:04:57.940 --> 00:05:00.990
in what happens in the point of no return.

00:05:00.990 --> 00:05:05.830
Be careful and don’t think that that makes
the movie not apply to The Nutshell Technique

00:05:05.830 --> 00:05:10.550
because it didn’t seem like the character
got what it wanted.

00:05:10.550 --> 00:05:16.199
I talk about it in GROUNDHOG DAY because it’s
a great example of this point.

00:05:16.199 --> 00:05:20.460
His most obvious of what he talks about in
the first scene.

00:05:20.460 --> 00:05:25.120
He thinks there’s a network that is scouting
him for a big network job.

00:05:25.120 --> 00:05:26.840
That’s his most obvious want.

00:05:26.840 --> 00:05:30.030
He wants a big network job.

00:05:30.030 --> 00:05:32.060
He doesn’t get that want.

00:05:32.060 --> 00:05:35.169
I’m not saying he doesn’t want it.

00:05:35.169 --> 00:05:37.650
He says he wants it, I believe him.

00:05:37.650 --> 00:05:39.740
It’s just not the set-up want.

00:05:39.740 --> 00:05:44.349
So you need to look at find something else
that the character wants that they get.

00:05:44.349 --> 00:05:49.259
So I’m not negating the fact that Phil Connors
wants a network job.

00:05:49.259 --> 00:05:52.780
I’m not negating the fact that Phil Connors
wants women to worship him.

00:05:52.780 --> 00:05:57.379
There are lots of things he’s not going
to get just like we all want things we don’t

00:05:57.379 --> 00:05:58.379
get.

00:05:58.379 --> 00:06:01.870
The thing that he wants that he gets is a
little throwaway line that he says.

00:06:01.870 --> 00:06:05.520
That he doesn’t want to spend more than
24 hours in Punxsutawney.

00:06:05.520 --> 00:06:07.060
It’s a little throwaway line.

00:06:07.060 --> 00:06:11.840
Matter of fact it wasn’t in the three versions
of the screenplay that I looked at researching

00:06:11.840 --> 00:06:13.910
for this book.

00:06:13.910 --> 00:06:20.430
It was very late in the process that they
also discovered “We are missing something.”

00:06:20.430 --> 00:06:30.520
We are missing something that makes Phil (even
Phil, this jerk) isn’t a victim of the Universe

00:06:30.520 --> 00:06:35.039
suddenly making him stuck on one day.

00:06:35.039 --> 00:06:39.069
He kind of in the original drafts was kind
of a victim, just this thing happened to him

00:06:39.069 --> 00:06:41.219
and he’s stuck.

00:06:41.219 --> 00:06:45.289
The writer is at a very latent process because
it’s not in the three scripts I’ve seen

00:06:45.289 --> 00:06:51.039
but some place between those three drafts
and the movie was shot, they added a scene

00:06:51.039 --> 00:06:57.280
where he says to the substitute weatherman
that he wants to spend more time covering

00:06:57.280 --> 00:07:00.979
for him when he’s in Punxsutawney.

00:07:00.979 --> 00:07:04.340
Phil says “Please, like I want to spend
an extra second in Punxsutawney?”

00:07:04.340 --> 00:07:07.300
It’s a throwaway line “I don’t want
to spend more than 24 hours in Punxsutawney.”

00:07:07.300 --> 00:07:13.530
We are just setting up, be careful of what
you wish for because he got his want.

00:07:13.530 --> 00:07:18.439
He only has to spend 24 hours in Punxsutawney,
right?

00:07:18.439 --> 00:07:20.280
Except for there’s a big catch.

00:07:20.280 --> 00:07:23.860
He’s going to have to spend the same 24
hours over and over again.

00:07:23.860 --> 00:07:25.320
So the want is a set-up want.

00:07:25.320 --> 00:07:27.689
I call it the “set-up want” with quotes
around it.

00:07:27.689 --> 00:07:29.820
It’s not necessarily the characters big
want.

00:07:29.820 --> 00:07:32.620
Film Courage: Can we use an example from CHINATOWN?

00:07:32.620 --> 00:07:33.639
Jill: Okay.

00:07:33.639 --> 00:07:38.000
Film Courage: Jack Nicholson’s character
and the initial want.

00:07:38.000 --> 00:07:40.830
He meets Faye Dunaway.

00:07:40.830 --> 00:07:45.340
What the first want that he gets that is not
really his real want?

00:07:45.340 --> 00:07:48.400
Jill: Well…I’ll tell you what the real
want is.

00:07:48.400 --> 00:07:53.690
So the set-up want we are going to see literally
in their first scene.

00:07:53.690 --> 00:07:55.880
So it’s before he meets Faye Dunaway.

00:07:55.880 --> 00:07:59.919
It would be in that very first scene where’s
he’s got Curly sitting across from him.

00:07:59.919 --> 00:08:07.970
Curly is the client showing pictures showing
that he’s been cheating on him right?

00:08:07.970 --> 00:08:12.039
And Curly is sobbing all over the place.

00:08:12.039 --> 00:08:15.310
So what he wants is what he doesn’t have
here.

00:08:15.310 --> 00:08:18.090
He wants a classic case.

00:08:18.090 --> 00:08:22.440
This cheating wives thing is not really classy.

00:08:22.440 --> 00:08:24.500
So what he wants is a classic case.

00:08:24.500 --> 00:08:27.120
He wants something classier than what he is
getting.

00:08:27.120 --> 00:08:32.719
He gets that in the main case that is going
to be Chinatown.

00:08:32.719 --> 00:08:36.390
The catch is it’s got corruption all over
it.

00:08:36.390 --> 00:08:39.530
It’s a lot bigger than he thought it was.

00:08:39.530 --> 00:08:43.720
Film Courage: It’s not even the wife.

00:08:43.720 --> 00:08:44.720
Jill: Right.

00:08:44.720 --> 00:08:45.720
Film Courage: Interesting.

00:08:45.720 --> 00:08:48.170
So that is his initial want.

00:08:48.170 --> 00:08:51.950
He thinks he gets it and he gets this femme
fatale that comes in and it turns out it’s

00:08:51.950 --> 00:08:53.750
a whole mess of trouble.

00:08:53.750 --> 00:08:55.050
Jill: Right.

00:08:55.050 --> 00:09:00.940
So the want right away is this throwaway thing
that they say.

00:09:00.940 --> 00:09:05.910
In JUNO it’s got another nice example I
talk about in the book that the want is a…it’s

00:09:05.910 --> 00:09:15.330
a really clever one actually where she’s
drinking the SunnyD because she’s taking

00:09:15.330 --> 00:09:17.560
the pregnancy test and she’s trying to pee.

00:09:17.560 --> 00:09:24.950
She’s drinking the SunnyD and she sees an
old discarded chair that somebody left out

00:09:24.950 --> 00:09:33.780
on the curb and that reminds her of the flashback
of her being intimate with Pauly (the moment

00:09:33.780 --> 00:09:36.560
that got her pregnant).

00:09:36.560 --> 00:09:41.890
The thing that she wants is she’s just looking
at a chair.

00:09:41.890 --> 00:09:47.190
She wants to save something that is being
discarded (the chair that she pulls out and

00:09:47.190 --> 00:09:50.850
puts on Pauly’s lawn is sort of this act,
right?

00:09:50.850 --> 00:09:54.960
And then how does she save something that
is being discarded at the point of no return?

00:09:54.960 --> 00:09:58.680
What’s the point of no return?

00:09:58.680 --> 00:10:03.020
Film Courage: …It’s been a few years.

00:10:03.020 --> 00:10:05.250
Something with her babysitting job?

00:10:05.250 --> 00:10:07.130
Jill: No.

00:10:07.130 --> 00:10:12.340
She’s pregnant and she’s planning to terminate
it and she can’t go through with it.

00:10:12.340 --> 00:10:18.450
So that is something that is about to be discarded,
a fetus.

00:10:18.450 --> 00:10:21.270
So what we’re doing is you’re getting
your want but you’re getting it with irony,

00:10:21.270 --> 00:10:22.270
right?

00:10:22.270 --> 00:10:25.640
In the beginning she just wanted to save a
chair, right?

00:10:25.640 --> 00:10:28.310
And I call it a throwaway want.

00:10:28.310 --> 00:10:35.460
It also fits with her character really nicely
as someone who kind of saves discarded things.

00:10:35.460 --> 00:10:38.040
She has a hamburger phone, you know?

00:10:38.040 --> 00:10:43.470
And people like Pauly who is sort of discarded,
so it’s very fitting in her character that

00:10:43.470 --> 00:10:46.340
she wants to save this discarded living room
set.

00:10:46.340 --> 00:10:51.840
But then she goes to save a fetus that she
thought she could discard and discovers that’s

00:10:51.840 --> 00:10:53.600
a lot harder than she thought it was going
to be.

